@startuml p_precreated
!include _style.puml
title P1–P4 — pre-created tickets: one row per seat, selling is an UPDATE

entity "event" as e {
  * event_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  * band_id : BIGINT <<FK>>
  * capacity : INT
  * description : TEXT
  <back:#D8F0DE>* seats_sold : INT  (P4 only, CHECK 0..capacity)</back>
}
entity "ticket  (capacity rows per event)" as t {
  * ticket_id : BIGINT <<PK>>
  --
  * event_id : BIGINT <<FK>>
  * seat_no : INT
  <back:#FFF3CD>* status : 'available' | 'sold'</back>
  customer_id : BIGINT
  sold_at : TIMESTAMPTZ
  --
  UNIQUE (event_id, seat_no)
  INDEX (event_id, seat_no) WHERE status = 'available'
}
e ||--|{ t

note right of t
  **P1 lock-first** (READ COMMITTED)
  SELECT … ORDER BY seat_no LIMIT 1 **FOR UPDATE**
  UPDATE … SET status='sold'
  Every buyer waits on the same lowest seat.

  **P2 skip-locked**
  SELECT … LIMIT 1 **FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED**
  UPDATE … SET status='sold'
  no row → EXISTS(available)? retry : sold out

  **P3 CAS** (autocommit, no lock)
  SELECT first available seat ≥ random start
  UPDATE … WHERE ticket_id=$ **AND status='available'**
  0 rows → lost the race, retry

  **P4 skip+counter**
  P2, then UPDATE event SET seats_sold+1
  — every buyer queues on the event row again
end note

note bottom of t
  <back:#FFCCCC> **The price of pre-creation** </back>: publishing a
  100 000-seat event inserts 100 000 rows before a
  single sale, and each sale leaves a dead entry in
  the partial index until vacuum removes it.
end note
@enduml
